module System/Concurrency

### A mailbox that jobs pass values through, in order: `Mailbox[T](capacity: 8)`, then `m.add_last(value: x)` on
### one side and `each x in m` (or `m.remove_first()`) on the other. Hand the same mailbox to every job that adds or
### takes.
###
### `add_last` waits while the mailbox is full, `each` and `remove_first` wait while it is empty. `close()` ends it,
### from either side: a later `add_last` crashes with `ChannelClosedError`, and `each` takes what is left and then
### ends. Nothing closes a mailbox by itself, so the side that is done calls `close()`. A mailbox nobody closes leaves
### its readers waiting, which the runtime reports as a deadlock instead of hanging quietly.
###
### Several jobs can take from one mailbox: each value goes to one of them.
### :typeparam T: The values the mailbox carries.
@self_locking
entity Mailbox[T]
relates RF::System.ChannelDrain[T] as Iter
obeys Iterable[T]
    ### The transmitting side, kept open until `close()`.
    secret transmitter: RF::System.Transmitter[T]
    ### The taking side, shared by every reader.
    secret receiver: RF::System.SharedReceiver[T]

### Makes an empty mailbox that holds up to `capacity` values before `add_last` waits (0: each `add_last` waits for
### a taker).
### :param capacity: How many values it holds.
### :returns: The new mailbox.
routine Mailbox[T](capacity: Integer) -> Mailbox[T]
    var (transmitter, receiver) = make_shared_channel[T](capacity: capacity.to[U64]())
    return Mailbox[T](transmitter: transmitter, receiver: receiver)

### Adds `value` at the end, waiting while the mailbox is full.
### :param value: The value that goes in.
### :throws: {ChannelClosedError} when the mailbox was closed.
failable routine Mailbox[T].add_last(value: T)
    me.transmitter.transmit(item: value)
    return

### Adds `value` at the end only when the mailbox has room for it now, never waiting: the step a broadcast takes per
### subscriber, where waiting on one full mailbox would hold up the rest. A mailbox of capacity 0 has room while no
### value waits in it.
### :param value: The value that goes in.
### :returns: `true` when it went in, `false` when the mailbox was full.
### :throws: {ChannelClosedError} when the mailbox was closed.
failable routine Mailbox[T].add_last_if_room(value: T) -> Bool
    return me.transmitter.transmit_if_room(item: value)

### Takes the first value out, waiting while the mailbox is empty.
### :returns: The value that was first.
### :throws: Crashes when the mailbox is closed and empty.
failable routine Mailbox[T].remove_first() -> T
    var drain = me.receiver.iter()
    return drain.emit()

### Ends the mailbox: a later `add_last` crashes, and `each` takes what is left and then ends.
routine Mailbox[T].close()
    me.transmitter.close()
    return

### Whether the mailbox was closed.
### :returns: `true` once `close()` ran.
routine Mailbox[T].is_closed() -> Bool
    return me.transmitter.is_closed()

### How many values wait in the mailbox right now. Another job can change it at once, so it is for showing, not
### for deciding.
### :returns: The number of values waiting.
routine Mailbox[T].count() -> Integer
    return me.receiver.count().to[Integer]()

### Takes values in order (`each x in m`), waiting while the mailbox is empty, until it is closed and empty.
### :returns: The walk over the mailbox.
routine Mailbox[T].iter() -> RF::System.ChannelDrain[T]
    return me.receiver.iter()
